{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>You shall not seek their peace</b> — from the implication of what is stated (Deuteronomy 23:17), \"he shall dwell with you in your midst,\" one might think this one too [may dwell]; therefore Scripture says, \"you shall not seek their peace.\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 23:7). And this midrash in the Sifrei (Tetzeh 251) concerns their welfare: from the implication of what is stated there, \"you shall not wrong him\" (lit. \"do not oppress him,\" lo tonenu, understood as: you shall not speak against him) — he excluded these from that welfare. But \"you shall not seek their peace\" is derived in the Sifrei from the fact that Scripture says (Deuteronomy 20:10), \"When you draw near to a city to fight against it, you shall proclaim peace to it\" — one might think even Ammon and Moab; therefore Scripture says, \"you shall not seek their peace.\" And so too in Tanhuma. And it appears to me that war against Ammon and Moab the Scripture has forbidden us for all generations, and the verse that says (Deuteronomy 2:9, 19) <b>do not provoke them</b> — this is not a commandment for that time only, but it is a negative commandment for all generations, as it says (there), \"for to the children of Lot I have given it as an inheritance.\" And behold, their land is theirs forever, for it is an inheritance that HaShem bequeathed to them. If so, the verse that says <b>you shall not seek</b> commands that if we wage war against them in a city that they themselves conquered from the nations, we shall not call out to them for peace. And likewise if they come to wage war in our land, it is permitted to us to pursue them and to conquer from them the provinces, to strike the inhabitants of the cities that did evil against us, as Jephthah did (Judges 11:33) — \"and he struck them from Aroer as far as your coming to Minnith, twenty cities\" — and so David did to all the cities of the children of Ammon (II Samuel 12:31), because they breached the fence first and waged war against him. And then we need not call for their peace, and we may strike those who fight against us in every city — the men, the women, and the children, if we wish. And they said in Midrash Rabbah (Numbers Rabbah 21:6): \"And you shall strike every fortified city and every choice city, and you shall fell every good tree, and you shall stop up every spring of water\" (II Kings 3:19). They said to him: the Scripture says (Deuteronomy 20:19), \"you shall not destroy its tree,\" and you say so? He said to them: concerning all the nations He commanded, but this one is contemptible and despised, as it says (II Kings 3:18), \"and this is a slight thing in the eyes of YHWH, and He will deliver Moab into your hand\" — for it says, \"you shall not seek their peace and their welfare\" — these are good trees. And all this is to harm them and to afflict them, but the land remains for that family, for it is an inheritance that HaShem bequeathed to them. And this matter is mentioned in the Aggadot in Genesis Rabbah (74:13) and in Midrash Tehillim (60:1). And by way of the plain sense, the reason for \"you shall not seek their peace and their welfare\" is as if to say: even though they are of your family and Abraham your father loved their father as a brother to him, a nursing child from his mother's breast, you shall not be to them as brothers who seek peace and welfare, for they violated the covenant of brotherhood, and it shall be void forever. And behold, HaShem preserved for the children of Lot the merit of their father, who accompanied His prophet on the way that YHWH sent him, and therefore He bequeathed to them from the inheritance of Abraham, and He punished His sons in their sin, that they would not cleave to Israel. And so the Egyptians He distanced three generations because of their wickedness, for they did many evils and troubles to us; but He did not abominate them forever, because we were sojourners in their land and we survived with them in the days of famine by the honor they showed to our father, and they appointed over them from us a ruler and governor. And so He did with Edom: He preserved for them the merit of the fathers, for they were of the seed of holiness, and He distanced them three generations because they came out to meet us with the sword and did not remember the covenant of brothers. And the tradition from our Rabbis (Yevamot 78a) is that there is no third generation from the days of Moses, but when one of them converts, his third generation may enter the congregation of HaShem."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The word טוב/טובתם in the midrashic derivation is read as 'welfare,' contrasting with the prohibition of seeking their שלום וטובתם; I have rendered it consistently as 'welfare' where it refers to the legal category, and as the plain sense requires otherwise. The citation 'במדב\"ר כא ו' is resolved as Numbers Rabbah 21:6."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:19.873811605Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}